/**
@mainpage IMS SDK

@tableofcontents

@section overview Overview

The IMS SDK provides the official public interface for integrating with the
IMS platform. It enables client applications, plugins, and external systems
to interact with IMS components in a stable and supported manner.

The SDK is designed for:

- High-performance native applications
- Hardware integration
- Automation systems
- External tooling and data processing pipelines

This documentation is generated directly from the distributed public headers,
ensuring alignment between the SDK reference and the shipped binaries.

---

@section version_info Version Information

<b>SDK Version:</b> \SDK_VERSION

The shared library follows semantic versioning:

- Linux: `libims.so.<major>.<minor>.<patch>`
- Windows: `imsLibrary.dll`

Backward compatibility is guaranteed within the same major version.

Debug builds are available with a 'd' suffix

---

@section installation Installation

The SDK distribution contains:

- `include/` — Public header files
- `lib/` — Import libraries and shared libraries

## Linux

Link against:

@code
-lims          (Release)
-limsd         (Debug)
@endcode

Ensure the shared library is available in:

- `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`, or
- `/usr/lib`, or
- application rpath

Example:

@code
g++ main.cpp -I/path/to/sdk/include -L/path/to/sdk/lib -lims
@endcode

---

## Windows

Link against:

- `imsLibrary.lib` (Release)
- `imsLibraryd.lib` (Debug)

Ensure `imsLibrary.dll` (or `imsLibraryd.dll`) is available on the system PATH or alongside your executable.

---

@section architecture Architecture

The SDK is organized into logical modules:

- Core API
- Configuration & Settings
- Device Control
- Data Acquisition
- Utility Services

Refer to the Modules page for detailed descriptions.

---

@section threading Threading Model

Unless explicitly stated:

- Public API functions are thread-safe.
- Individual object instances are not implicitly synchronized.
- The caller is responsible for concurrency control where required.

---

@section memory Memory Ownership

The API follows strict ownership conventions:

- Functions that allocate memory document ownership explicitly.
- Objects created via factory functions must be destroyed using matching API calls.
- Caller-provided buffers must meet documented size requirements.

Failure to adhere to documented ownership rules results in undefined behavior.

---

@section error_handling Error Handling

Errors are reported via:

- Return codes
- Status/result objects
- Exceptions (C++ interfaces where applicable)

All public API functions document possible failure modes.

---

@section support Support & Compatibility

This SDK version is intended for use with the corresponding IMS runtime.

Mixing SDK and runtime versions across major revisions is not supported.

For integration guidance, refer to the module documentation and example usage
provided within the header reference.

---

@section generated Documentation Notes

This documentation is automatically generated using Doxygen
from the public headers shipped with the SDK.

Only public API symbols are included. Internal implementation
details are intentionally excluded.

Generated on: \date
*/
